I have two controllers and one service:

GreetingWebController — returns a web page (HTML view)
GreetingRestController — returns raw data (text or JSON)
GreetingAppService — contains the actual business logic

Difference between the two controllers:

| Controller               | Annotation        | Purpose                                   | URL Example  | Return Type                                      |
| ------------------------ | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------------ | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `GreetingWebController`  | `@Controller`     | Serves a web **view (HTML)** for browsers | `/`          | returns `"index"` → a template like `index.html` |
| `GreetingRestController` | `@RestController` | Serves **data (JSON / text)** for APIs    | `/api/greet` | returns raw text `"Hello, ..."`                  |

In short:
@Controller → for web pages (frontend)
@RestController → for REST APIs (backend / API clients)

Visiting http://localhost:8080/ → renders index.html with the greeting
Visiting http://localhost:8080/api/greet?name=Reza → returns text or JSON response



They are decoupled — they do not affect each other because:

Each listens on its own path (/ vs /api/greet)
They both use the same service layer, but independently

GreetingAppService is the business logic layer.
It’s not mandatory — we could move its logic into the controller, but that’s bad practice.




Why it’s good to keep it:
*Separation of concerns
Controllers handle HTTP requests; services handle logic.
*Reusability
Both controllers can use the same greeting logic — as they do right now.
*Testability
we can unit-test GreetingAppService without loading the web context.




So:
Technically: we can delete it.
Practically: we shouldn’t — it’s good design.



| Component                | Role               | Can Delete?                  | Affects Others?               |
| ------------------------ | ------------------ | ---------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| `GreetingWebController`  | Web page (HTML UI) | Only if we don’t need HTML  | No                            |
| `GreetingRestController` | REST API (data)    | Only if we don’t need API   | No                            |
| `GreetingAppService`     | Business logic     | we can, but not recommended | Both controllers depend on it |
